<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!-- launchd SYSTEM DAEMON template for the PearCircle seeder, installed to
     /Library/LaunchDaemons/com.pearcircle.seeder.plist by the .pkg post-install
     script. Everything __LIKE_THIS__ is templated at install time.

     A SYSTEM DAEMON, NOT A LAUNCHAGENT, and that is the whole point of this file.

     This used to be a LaunchAgent in ~/Library/LaunchAgents, and its comment
     claimed "the daemon survives logout via KeepAlive". That was false. An agent
     there loads into gui/501, a domain whose header reads `type = login`,
     `creator = loginwindow` - loginwindow builds it at login and tears it down at
     logout. KeepAlive restarts a job that EXITS; it does not exempt one from its
     domain's teardown. Measured on the mac-mini 2026-07-31 with a heartbeat agent
     across one real logout/login: last beat `21:58:04Z pid=12235`, then 48s of
     nothing, then `21:58:52Z pid=13652`. Killed at logout; a DIFFERENT process
     started at login. A crash-plus-KeepAlive would have resumed in seconds and
     would not have waited for the login.

     macOS also refuses to bootstrap an arbitrary job into user/501 (error 5), so
     it is the GUI session or the system domain, with nothing in between.

     RUN AS THE USER, NOT AS ROOT - while still being a system-domain daemon.
     Those are independent: what survives logout is living in the system domain
     rather than gui/501, and UserName only decides which credentials the job runs
     with. This matters because the data directory stays exactly where it has
     always been, under the user's ~/Library/Application Support. Running as root
     instead would leave root-owned files inside the user's own store - PearTune
     hit precisely that on 2026-08-08 and had to back it out, because it left the
     unprivileged app unable to write files it supposedly owned. Keeping the
     credentials means the store is untouched, so this change is invisible to the
     seeder itself and to every peer it talks to.

     Same pattern, already proven on this hardware: PearTune's
     com.peerloom.peartune daemon, which survived a real reboot with nobody
     logged in. -->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.pearcircle.seeder</string>

  <!-- THE DATA DIR IS THE SEEDER'S IDENTITY, so it is passed EXPLICITLY rather
       than derived. host/dataDir.js resolves it from os.homedir(), which reads
       $HOME and only falls back to the passwd entry - and launchd does not give a
       system daemon the same HOME an agent inherits from the login session. Left
       to chance, the daemon would come up on a different, empty store and the
       seeder would look brand new to every peer that knows it. Passing the data
       dir as an argument removes the guesswork; the HOME below covers anything
       else that reads the home directory. Same reasoning as PEARTUNE_DATA in
       com.peerloom.peartune.

       (Note for anyone editing this comment: a double hyphen cannot appear inside
       an XML comment, so the flag names here are spelled out in prose. Writing it
       literally makes the plist unparseable and the installer fails.) -->
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/usr/local/lib/pearcircle-seeder/pearcircle-seeder</string>
    <string>--no-open</string>
    <string>--data-dir</string>
    <string>__DATA_DIR__</string>
  </array>

  <!-- See the UserName note above: system domain for the lifetime, user
       credentials so the data directory and its ownership are unchanged. -->
  <key>UserName</key>
  <string>__USER__</string>
  <key>GroupName</key>
  <string>__GROUP__</string>

  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>HOME</key>
    <string>__USER_HOME__</string>
  </dict>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>

  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <true/>

  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>__LOG_PATH__</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>__LOG_PATH__</string>

  <key>ProcessType</key>
  <string>Background</string>
</dict>
</plist>
